var definitions = ['<h2>Un vaso de leche.</h2><div><strong>Un</strong> This is the Spanish indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>vaso</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "glass" or "cup". It is a masculine singular noun that indicates a container used for drinking liquids.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a Spanish preposition commonly used to indicate "of" in English. It shows the relationship between "vaso" and "leche", expressing possession or what the glass contains.</div><div><strong>leche</strong> This is a Spanish noun that means "milk". It’s a feminine singular noun that denotes the liquid that is contained in the glass in this phrase.</div>','<h2>Una película de acción</h2><div><strong>Una</strong> This is the Spanish indefinite article for the feminine singular noun that follows. It translates to "a" or "an" in English. It is used here to refer to any single movie within the category of action movies.</div><div><strong>película</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "movie" or "film". It is feminine and singular, matching the article "una".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that expresses possession or relationship between elements. In this context, it is used to link "película" to the type of film, which is "acción", translating to "of" or "about" in English.</div><div><strong>acción</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "action". It is used here as a descriptive term to specify the genre of the movie. It represents films characterized by dynamic sequences, fast-paced plots, and often, physical stunts or combat.</div>','<h2>Un jugo de manzana</h2><div><strong>Un</strong> This is the Spanish indefinite article for a singular, masculine noun, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>jugo</strong> This is the Spanish word for "juice". It is a masculine noun in its singular form.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that means "of" or "from". It is used here to indicate the content or what the juice is made of.</div><div><strong>manzana</strong> This is the Spanish word for "apple". It is a feminine noun in its singular form, and here it denotes the type of juice being referenced.</div>','<h2>Estoy cansada de caminar.</h2><div><strong>Estoy</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "estar", which means "to be" in English. It indicates a temporary state or condition.</div><div><strong>cansada</strong> This is a Spanish adjective meaning "tired". If the speaker were male, it would be "cansado". It agrees in gender and number with the person who is speaking. In this case, it suggests that the speaker is a female saying she is tired.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can translate to "of", "from", "about", and other prepositions in English. In this context, it is used to link the adjective "cansada" to the action of walking, to express being tired of that action.</div><div><strong>caminar</strong> This is the infinitive form of the Spanish verb that means "to walk". It’s used here to specify the action that is causing the tiredness.</div>','<h2>El cuarto de baño</h2><div><strong>El</strong> This is the definite article in Spanish, equivalent to "the" in English. It is used here to specify a particular room.</div><div><strong>cuarto</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "room". In the context of this sentence, it’s indicating a particular type of room within a house or building.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can translate to "of" or "from" in English. In this sentence, it’s used to link "cuarto" and "baño" to specify the type of room.</div><div><strong>baño</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "bath" or "bathroom". When combined with "cuarto de", it refers to the "bathroom" or literally "room of bath".</div>','<h2>El libro de Mateo</h2><div><strong>El</strong> This is the definite article in Spanish for masculine, singular nouns, similar to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>libro</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "book". It is a masculine, singular noun.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a Spanish preposition typically meaning "of" or "from". In this context, it is used to indicate possession or belonging.</div><div><strong>Mateo</strong> This is a Spanish proper noun, a masculine given name equivalent to "Matthew" in English. In this context, it likely refers to the person who owns or is associated with the book.</div>','<h2>Diego viene de Puebla.</h2><div><strong>Diego</strong> This is a personal name, typically a masculine given name in Spanish-speaking countries.</div><div><strong>viene</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "venir", which means "to come". In this sentence, it indicates Diego’s action or movement from somewhere.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can have various meanings including "of", "from", or "about". In this case, it’s used to indicate the origin or place where Diego comes from.</div><div><strong>Puebla</strong> This is a proper noun which refers to a city/state in Mexico. It is the location Diego is coming from.</div>','<h2>Pedro sale de Monterrey.</h2><div><strong>Pedro</strong> This is a proper noun, a masculine given name used here as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>sale</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "salir", which means "to leave" or "to go out". In this sentence, it conveys that Pedro is leaving or departing from somewhere.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a Spanish preposition that can mean "of", "from", or "about", among other things. In this context, it is used to indicate the starting point of the action, i.e., where Pedro is leaving from.</div><div><strong>Monterrey</strong> This is a proper noun, referring to a city in Mexico. In this sentence, it is the location Pedro is leaving from.</div>','<h2>Ella viene de Madrid.</h2><div><strong>Ella</strong> This is the Spanish third-person singular female pronoun, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>viene</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "venir", which means "to come". In this context, it indicates the subject’s action of coming from somewhere.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a Spanish preposition that is often used to indicate origin, possession, or source. In this sentence, it is used to mean "from", showing the place the subject comes from.</div><div><strong>Madrid</strong> This is a proper noun, the capital city of Spain. It indicates the place where the subject comes from.</div>','<h2>Soy un estudiante del colegio.</h2><div><strong>Soy</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "ser", which means "to be". It describes the state of being or identity of the subject.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the indefinite article for masculine singular nouns in Spanish, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>estudiante</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "student". It can refer to a person who is studying at a school, college, or university.</div><div><strong>del</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "de" ("of" or "from" in English) and the definite article "el" ("the" for masculine singular nouns). It indicates a relationship of possession, typically translating to "of the" or "from the" in English.</div><div><strong>colegio</strong> This is a Spanish noun that means "school" or "college". It typically refers to an educational institution for children or teenagers.</div>','<h2>Esta tiza es del maestro.</h2><div><strong>Esta</strong> This is a demonstrative adjective in Spanish that means "this". It is used here to indicate that the chalk (tiza) is close to the speaker and is feminine singular to agree with the noun "tiza".</div><div><strong>tiza</strong> This is the Spanish word for "chalk". It’s a feminine singular noun referred to by the preceding demonstrative adjective "esta".</div><div><strong>es</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the Spanish verb "ser", which means "to be". It is used here to link the subject "Esta tiza" with the predicate "del maestro".</div><div><strong>del</strong> This is a contraction of the Spanish preposition "de" (meaning "of" or "from") and the masculine singular definite article "el" (meaning "the"). It’s used here to indicate possession or relationship, effectively meaning "of the" or "belonging to the".</div><div><strong>maestro</strong> This is a Spanish noun that means "teacher" or "master". It is masculine singular and the owner of the chalk in this sentence.</div>','<h2>El chef del restaurante</h2><div><strong>El</strong> This is the definite article in Spanish, equivalent to "the" in English. It indicates that the following noun is masculine singular.</div><div><strong>chef</strong> This noun is borrowed from French, meaning the head cook in a restaurant. It is used in many languages to refer to someone with expert cooking skills who is in charge of a kitchen’s operations.</div><div><strong>del</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "de" and the definite article "el", translating to "of the" or "from the" in English. It shows a relationship of possession or association between the chef and the restaurant.</div><div><strong>restaurante</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in Spanish, and it means "restaurant". It refers to a place where people pay to sit and eat meals that are cooked and served on the premises.</div>'];var defBonus = ['<h2>¿Qué tipo de música te gusta?</h2><div><strong>¿Qué</strong> This is the Spanish word for "what". Here, it begins a question that is asking for specific information.</div><div><strong>tipo</strong> This Spanish noun means "type" or "kind". In the context of this question, it is inquiring about a variety or category.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a Spanish preposition usually translated as "of" in English. It is used here to link "tipo" (type) with "música" (music), indicating what kind of category is being asked about.</div><div><strong>música</strong> This is the Spanish word for "music". It’s the object of the preposition "de", forming the phrase "tipo de música" which translates to "type of music".</div><div><strong>te</strong> This is a Spanish reflexive or indirect object pronoun that means "you". It indicates to whom the like or preference (gusta) is directed, which is the second-person singular.</div><div><strong>gusta</strong> This is a third-person singular form of the Spanish verb "gustar", which means "to like". Unlike in English, in Spanish, the thing that is liked is the subject of the verb, and the person who likes it is indicated by an indirect object pronoun (in this case "te" for "you"). The construction literally translates to "What type of music is pleasing to you?" but the natural English equivalent is "What type of music do you like?"</div>','<h2>¿Qué tipo de habitación quiere?</h2><div><strong>¿Qué</strong> This word translates to "what" in English. It is an interrogative pronoun used here to form a question about the nature or identity of something.</div><div><strong>tipo</strong> This word means "type" in English. It is used to refer to a category or classification of things.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This word translates to "of" in English. It is a preposition used here to indicate possession or relation.</div><div><strong>habitación</strong> This word means "room" in English. In this context, it refers to a room in a lodging or accommodation setting like a hotel.</div><div><strong>quiere</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "querer", which means "to want" or "to wish". It’s used to inquire about the preferences or desires of the person being addressed.</div>','<h2>Es el último día de clases.</h2><div><strong>Es</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "ser", which means "to be". It describes the state of being or identity of the subject, in this case, referring to "it" (the day).</div><div><strong>el</strong> This is the definite article in Spanish, masculine singular form, which means "the". It is used here to refer to "día" (day).</div><div><strong>último</strong> This is a Spanish adjective meaning "last" or "final". It agrees in gender and number with the noun it describes, which is "día" (day) in this instance.</div><div><strong>día</strong> This word means "day" in Spanish. It is a masculine noun in singular form.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that translates to "of" in English. It is used here to link "día" with "clases", showing a possessive or descriptive relationship.</div><div><strong>clases</strong> This is the plural form of the Spanish noun "clase", which means "class" or "lesson". Here, it refers to school classes or courses collectively.</div>','<h2>Estamos enfrente de la casa.</h2><div><strong>Estamos</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "estar", which means "to be". It is used to describe temporary states or locations. Here, it implies "we are".</div><div><strong>enfrente</strong> This is a Spanish adverb meaning "in front of" or "opposite". It’s used to describe the position or location relative to something.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This word is a Spanish preposition usually translated as "of" or "from". In this context, it is part of the prepositional phrase "enfrente de" which means "in front of".</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the feminine singular definite article "the" in English. It’s used here to modify "casa" (house).</div><div><strong>casa</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "house". In this sentence, it is the object of the preposition "de", creating the meaning "in front of the house".</div>','<h2>Estoy enfrente de la escuela.</h2><div><strong>Estoy</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "estar", which means "to be" in English. Specifically, "estar" is used to describe temporary states or locations. Here, it indicates the speaker’s current location or position.</div><div><strong>enfrente</strong> This is a Spanish adverb meaning "in front of" or "facing". It indicates the position or direction relative to a point of reference, which in this sentence is the school.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a Spanish preposition that can be translated as "of" or "from" in English. In this context, it’s used to indicate a relationship of proximity, meaning "of" as in "in front of".</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the singular, feminine definite article in Spanish, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the noun "escuela", agreeing in gender and number.</div><div><strong>escuela</strong> This is a Spanish noun that means "school". It is a singular, feminine noun, which is why it is preceded by the feminine article "la".</div>','<h2>¿Quieres un pedazo de pastel?</h2><div><strong>¿Quieres</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "querer", which means "to want". In this form, it’s used to ask if someone wants something, in this case, it’s addressing "you" in an informal way.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the indefinite article in Spanish, for the masculine singular. It translates to "a" or "an" in English. It precedes a masculine singular noun.</div><div><strong>pedazo</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in Spanish that means "piece" or "slice". In this context, it refers to a piece of a whole item, which is a cake.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that is often translated as "of" in English. It indicates possession or a part of something.</div><div><strong>pastel</strong> This is a masculine singular noun in Spanish that means "cake". It is the object of the sentence, the item that a piece is being offered from.</div>','<h2>Ella quiere salir de su cuna.</h2><div><strong>Ella</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>quiere</strong> This comes from the verb "querer", which means "to want" or "to wish". In this form, it’s the third-person singular of the present indicative, meaning "she wants".</div><div><strong>salir</strong> This is an infinitive verb in Spanish meaning "to leave" or "to go out". Here, it indicates the action she wants to take.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can have various meanings including "of", "from", "about". In this context, it means "from".</div><div><strong>su</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "his", "her", "its", or "their" depending on context. Here it refers to "her", denoting possession of the noun that follows.</div><div><strong>cuna</strong> This is a noun meaning "crib" or "cradle". In this sentence, it’s the object that she wants to leave.</div>','<h2>Ella fue víctima de un robo.</h2><div><strong>Ella</strong> This is the third-person singular feminine pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "she" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>fue</strong> This is the third-person singular past tense form of the verb "ser", which means "to be". It describes the state of being or identity of the subject in the past.</div><div><strong>víctima</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "victim".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a Spanish preposition that can have several meanings depending on context. In this sentence, it is used to indicate the "victim of" something.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the indefinite article in Spanish, masculine singular form, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>robo</strong> This Spanish noun means "robbery" or "theft". It is a masculine singular noun in this context.</div>','<h2>El lago está enfrente de ellos.</h2><div><strong>El</strong> This is the Spanish definite article for the masculine singular noun, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>lago</strong> This is a Spanish masculine singular noun which means "lake".</div><div><strong>está</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "estar", which means "to be" in a temporary or locational context in Spanish. It indicates the current state or location.</div><div><strong>enfrente</strong> This is an adverb in Spanish that means "in front" or "opposite".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can mean "of", "from", "about" or "by" depending on the context. In this sentence, it is used to indicate a relationship of proximity.</div><div><strong>ellos</strong> This is the third-person plural pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "them" in English. It’s used here to refer to a group of people that the lake is in front of.</div>','<h2>¿Cuál es el precio de este auto?</h2><div><strong>¿Cuál</strong> This is a Spanish interrogative pronoun that means "which" or "what". It is used here to ask for a specific piece of information.</div><div><strong>es</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "ser", which means "to be". It links the subject with the attribute that follows, in this case, a price.</div><div><strong>el</strong> This is the Spanish definite article for masculine singular nouns, which translates to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>precio</strong> This is a Spanish noun that means "price". It refers to the cost or value of something, which is being questioned in this sentence.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a Spanish preposition that generally means "of" or "from". Here, it is used to relate the price to the car in question.</div><div><strong>este</strong> This is the Spanish demonstrative adjective for a masculine singular noun, which means "this". It specifies the object that is close to the speaker, in this case, the car ("auto").</div><div><strong>auto</strong> This is a Spanish noun that means "car" or "automobile". It is short for "automóvil", and it’s the item whose price is being inquired about.</div><div><strong>?</strong> This punctuation marks the sentence as an interrogative, indicating that a question is being asked.</div>','<h2>Me gusta el olor de estas flores.</h2><div><strong>Me</strong> This is the first-person singular indirect object pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "me" in English. Here, it is used to indicate that the speaker is the person who likes the smell.</div><div><strong>gusta</strong> This is the third-person singular form of the verb "gustar", which means "to like" or "to be pleasing to". In Spanish, when talking about liking something, "gustar" is used in a construction where the thing liked is the subject of the sentence and the person who likes it is the indirect object.</div><div><strong>el olor</strong> This phrase means "the smell" in English. "El" is the masculine singular definite article "the", and "olor" is the noun "smell". Together, they refer to the specific smell of something, in this case, the flowers.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can have various meanings, including "of", "from", and "about". Here, it’s used to link "the smell" to the source, which is "these flowers".</div><div><strong>estas</strong> This is the feminine plural form of the demonstrative adjective "this" in English. It’s used to point out specific things near the speaker.</div><div><strong>flores</strong> This is the Spanish word for "flowers". It is a noun in its plural form, matching the plural form of "estas". Therefore, it talks about multiple flowers.</div>','<h2>Tengo muy pocas fotos de mi viaje.</h2><div><strong>Tengo</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "tener", which means "to have" in English. It indicates possession or ownership.</div><div><strong>muy</strong> This is an adverb meaning "very" in English. It’s used here to intensify the adjective that follows.</div><div><strong>pocas</strong> This is a feminine plural form of the adjective "poco", which means "few" or "little" in English. It’s used to indicate a small quantity.</div><div><strong>fotos</strong> This is the plural form of "foto", which is short for "fotografía" and means "photos" in English. It’s the object of the sentence, the thing that the speaker has few of.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition meaning "of" or "from" in English. It’s used to indicate possession, origin, or the content of something.</div><div><strong>mi</strong> This is a possessive adjective meaning "my" in English. It indicates ownership of the following noun.</div><div><strong>viaje</strong> This is a masculine noun meaning "trip" or "journey" in English. It’s the thing of which the photos were taken.</div>','<h2>¿Quiere tomar una foto de nosotros?</h2><div><strong>¿Quiere</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "querer", which means "to want". In the context of the sentence, it’s used to politely ask someone if they want to do something, which translates to "Do you want" in English.</div><div><strong>tomar</strong> This is the infinitive form of a verb meaning "to take". In this sentence, it’s connected to "una foto", which means "a photo", to create the phrase "to take a photo".</div><div><strong>una foto</strong> This is a noun phrase in Spanish. "Una" is the feminine singular indefinite article "a", and "foto" is the abbreviation of "fotografía", which means "photo". Together they mean "a photo".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can mean many things depending on the context; here, it denotes possession or relation and is equivalent to "of" in English.</div><div><strong>nosotros?</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in Spanish, which translates to "us" in English. In the context of this sentence, it specifies that the photo is to be taken of the speaker and the group they are included in.</div>','<h2>La niñita bebe un vaso de leche.</h2><div><strong>La</strong> This is the definite article in Spanish for feminine nouns in the singular form, equivalent to "the" in English.</div><div><strong>niñita</strong> This is a diminutive form of the Spanish noun "niña", which means "little girl". It is used to denote affection or smallness.</div><div><strong>bebe</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "beber", which means "to drink". It indicates that the subject (the little girl) is performing the action of drinking.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the indefinite article in Spanish for masculine nouns in the singular form, equivalent to "a" or "an" in English. Here it precedes "vaso", which is masculine.</div><div><strong>vaso</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "glass". It is the object of the verb "beber", indicating what the little girl is drinking from.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can mean various things depending on the context. In this case, it is used to indicate the composition of the contents of the glass, equivalent to "of" in English.</div><div><strong>leche</strong> This is the Spanish word for "milk". It complements the preposition "de" to indicate what is in the glass that the little girl is drinking.</div>','<h2>Ella lavará el interior de su horno.</h2><div><strong>Ella</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>lavará</strong> This is the third-person singular future tense form of the verb "lavar", which means "to wash" or "to clean". Here, it indicates a future action that she will perform.</div><div><strong>el interior</strong> This phrase means "the interior" in English. It’s used here to specify the part of the oven that will be cleaned.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can be translated as "of" or "from" in English. In this case, it indicates possession.</div><div><strong>su</strong> This is a possessive adjective in Spanish that can mean "his", "her", "its", "your" (formal singular), or "their" depending on context. Here, it is used to indicate that the oven belongs to someone else not specified in the sentence, but since "Ella" is the subject, it likely implies "her oven".</div><div><strong>horno.</strong> This is a noun that means "oven" in English.</div>','<h2>Él felicitó al ganador de la carrera.</h2><div><strong>Él</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "he" in English. It’s used as the subject of the sentence, referring to a male individual.</div><div><strong>felicitó</strong> This is the third-person singular past tense form of the verb "felicitar", which means "to congratulate". It indicates that the subject (he) congratulated someone in the past.</div><div><strong>al</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "a" and the definite article "el", which translates as "to the" in English. It is used here to indicate direction or the recipient of the action.</div><div><strong>ganador</strong> This word translates to "winner" in English. It’s a noun referring to the person who won the race.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can mean "of", "from", "by", among other translations. Here, it is used to link "winner" to the following noun, indicating possession or association.</div><div><strong>la carrera</strong> These words together mean "the race" in English, with "la" being the feminine singular definite article "the", and "carrera" being a feminine singular noun meaning "race".</div>','<h2>Él trabaja en medio de ninguna parte.</h2><div><strong>Él</strong> This is the third-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "he" in English. It is the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>trabaja</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "trabajar", which means "to work". It indicates the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>en</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can be translated to "in" or "at" in English. It is used here to indicate the location related to the verb.</div><div><strong>medio</strong> This word means "middle" or "half" in Spanish. Here, it’s part of an expression.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is another preposition in Spanish, typically translated as "of" or "from" in English. It’s used here to link "medio" with "ninguna parte" to form an expression.</div><div><strong>ninguna parte</strong> This phrase means "nowhere" in English. It’s a negation that denotes an unspecified or nonexistent place.</div>','<h2>No está acostumbrada a este tipo de calor.</h2><div><strong>No</strong> This is the Spanish word for "no". It negates a statement or responds negatively to a question or statement.</div><div><strong>está</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "estar", which means "to be" in English. In this context, it’s used to convey the state or condition someone is in.</div><div><strong>acostumbrada</strong> This is the feminine singular form of the past participle "acostumbrado", which means "accustomed" or "used to" in English. Because it’s in the feminine form, it indicates that the subject is female.</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can be translated to "to" in English. It’s used here to connect the verb "acostumbrada" with the clause that follows, indicating what someone is not accustomed to.</div><div><strong>este</strong> This is the masculine singular form of the demonstrative adjective "este", which means "this" in English. It’s used to specify the type of heat being referred to.</div><div><strong>tipo</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "type" or "kind" in English.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can be translated as "of" in English. It’s used here to link the noun "tipo" and "calor" to mean "type of heat".</div><div><strong>calor</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "heat" in English. It refers to the temperature or hotness that the subject is not used to.</div>','<h2>Yo llego de la escuela.</h2><div><strong>Yo</strong> This is the first-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "I" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>llego</strong> This is the first-person singular present tense form of the verb "llegar", which means "to arrive". It states the action being performed by the subject.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can be translated as "from" in English. In this context, it indicates the origin or starting point of the action.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is the feminine singular definite article in Spanish, equivalent to "the" in English. It is used here to specify the school being referred to.</div><div><strong>escuela</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "school". It is a feminine singular noun, which is why it’s preceded by the feminine singular definite article "la".</div>','<h2>Sí. Está muy cerca de aquí.</h2><div><strong>Sí</strong> This is the Spanish word for "yes". It affirms a statement or responds positively to a question.</div><div><strong>Está</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "estar", which means "to be" in a location or a temporary state. It describes where something or someone is located.</div><div><strong>muy</strong> This is an adverb in Spanish meaning "very". It is used to enhance the adjective that follows it, indicating a higher degree of the adjective’s quality.</div><div><strong>cerca</strong> This word means "close" in English. As an adverb, it describes proximity to a reference point.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish meaning "of" or "from". In this sentence, it is used to link "cerca" with "aquí" to describe the location’s relation.</div><div><strong>aquí</strong> This is an adverb meaning "here" in English. It indicates the location as being in the immediate vicinity of the speaker or the context of the conversation.</div>','<h2>¡Ni modo! ¿Está lejos de aquí?</h2><div><strong>¡Ni modo!</strong> This is a Spanish expression that loosely translates to "Oh well!", "Such is life!", or "It can’t be helped!" It expresses a sense of resignation or acceptance that things are out of one’s control and cannot be changed.</div><div><strong>¿Está</strong> This is the third-person singular of the verb "estar" in the present indicative, which means "to be" in English. It’s used here to ask about the location or state of something.</div><div><strong>lejos</strong> This is a Spanish adverb meaning "far". It’s used to inquire about the distance of something from a certain point.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This a preposition in Spanish that can be translated as "from" or "of" among other functions. In this context, it’s part of the phrase that means "from here".</div><div><strong>aquí?</strong> This is a Spanish adverb meaning "here". It’s used in this question to specify the reference point for the distance being asked about.</div>','<h2>Tarda de tres a cinco días.</h2><div><strong>Tarda</strong> Es la tercera persona del singular del presente de indicativo del verbo "tardar", que significa "to take time" o "to delay" en inglés. Indica la duración de tiempo que se toma en realizar una acción.</div><div><strong>de</strong> Esta es una preposición que se utiliza para indicar el origen, la procedencia, la materia u otras relaciones. En este caso, se usa para establecer el inicio de un rango de tiempo.</div><div><strong>tres</strong> Este es un numeral que indica la cantidad "three" en inglés. Especifica la cantidad mínima de días en el rango de tiempo mencionado.</div><div><strong>a</strong> Esta es una preposición que comunmente indica dirección o límite. Aquí se emplea para delimitar el rango de tiempo, conectando el inicio con el final del intervalo.</div><div><strong>cinco</strong> Este es otro numeral que significa "five" en inglés. Indica el límite máximo de días en el rango de tiempo mencionado.</div><div><strong>días</strong> Es la forma plural de "día", que significa "days" en inglés. Refiere al periodo temporal al que se aplica la duración mencionada.</div>','<h2>No, está bastante cerca de aquí.</h2><div><strong>No</strong> This is the Spanish word for "no". It negates a statement or responds negatively to a question.</div><div><strong>está</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "estar", which means "to be". It is indicating a state or condition, such as location in this case.</div><div><strong>bastante</strong> This word translates to "quite" or "fairly" in English. It is an adverb used to modify adjectives or other adverbs, indicating a considerable degree or extent.</div><div><strong>cerca</strong> This is a Spanish adverb meaning "close" or "nearby". In this context, it refers to proximity.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can mean "of", "from", or "about". Here, it is used to relate "cerca" to the location being discussed.</div><div><strong>aquí</strong> This word means "here" in Spanish. It denotes the place where the speaker is currently located or the place being referred to that is close by.</div>','<h2>La nieve se caía de los árboles.</h2><div><strong>La</strong> This is the definite article in Spanish, meaning "the". It is feminine and singular to agree with the noun it modifies, "nieve".</div><div><strong>nieve</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "snow". It is feminine and singular here and serves as the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>se</strong> This is a reflexive pronoun which, in this case, is used as part of a reflexive verb construction that indicates the action is performed by the subject onto itself, or that the action is unintentional or passive.</div><div><strong>caía</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "caer", which means "to fall". The imperfect tense indicates that the action was an ongoing event in the past.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can mean "from" or "of". Here, it is used to indicate origin or the point where something starts.</div><div><strong>los</strong> This is the plural masculine definite article in Spanish, meaning "the". It matches with the plural, masculine noun "árboles".</div><div><strong>árboles</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "trees". It is masculine and plural, which is why it is preceded by the plural masculine definite article "los".</div>','<h2>Ella vino de muy lejos para ver esto.</h2><div><strong>Ella</strong> This is the third-person singular feminine pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vino</strong> This is the third-person singular past tense form of the verb "venir", which means "to come". It indicates that the action of coming was completed in the past.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish. It is used here to indicate origin or coming from somewhere.</div><div><strong>muy</strong> This is an adverb meaning "very" in English. It’s used here to intensify the following word, "lejos".</div><div><strong>lejos</strong> This is an adverb meaning "far" in English. It describes the distance from which she came.</div><div><strong>para</strong> This is a preposition that means "for" in English. It is used to indicate the purpose of an action.</div><div><strong>ver</strong> This is an infinitive verb that means "to see" in English. It indicates the action that she came to do.</div><div><strong>esto</strong> This is a demonstrative pronoun meaning "this" in English. It refers to an object, situation, or event that is being shown or discussed.</div>','<h2>Pronto, tú vas a volver de Argentina.</h2><div><strong>Pronto</strong> This is a Spanish adverb meaning "soon". It indicates that an action will take place in the near future.</div><div><strong>tú</strong> This is the second-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "you" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>vas</strong> This is the second-person singular present tense form of the verb "ir", which means "to go". In this context, it is part of the future construction "vas a" which means "you are going to".</div><div><strong>a</strong> This is a preposition used with "ir" in the construction "ir a" which is used to indicate a future action, equivalent to "going to" in English.</div><div><strong>volver</strong> This is an infinitive verb in Spanish meaning "to return". It is used here after "vas a" to indicate the action "you are going to return".</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can mean "from" among other things. It is used here to denote the origin of the action of returning.</div><div><strong>Argentina</strong> This is a proper noun indicating the country Argentina in Spanish. It is the place from which the subject will return.</div>','<h2>Sí, hay un parque muy lindo a dos cuadras de aquí.</h2><div><strong>Sí</strong> This is the Spanish word for "yes". It is used to affirm or agree with a statement or question.</div><div><strong>hay</strong> This is the third-person singular form of the verb "haber" in the present indicative, which means "there is" or "there are". It is used here to indicate the existence of something.</div><div><strong>un</strong> This is the Spanish indefinite article for masculine singular nouns, which translates to "a" or "an" in English.</div><div><strong>parque</strong> This is a masculine noun in Spanish which means "park".</div><div><strong>muy</strong> This is an adverb meaning "very" in English. It intensifies the adjective that follows it.</div><div><strong>lindo</strong> This is an adjective in Spanish, meaning "beautiful" or "nice". Here it describes the park, and it’s in the masculine singular form to agree with "parque".</div><div><strong>a dos cuadras</strong> This phrase means "two blocks" in English. "A" in this context could be understood as "at a distance of", and "cuadras" are blocks as urban distance measurement.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition which can mean "of", "from", among other concepts. In this context, it indicates the origin or starting point, which is "aquí" or "here".</div><div><strong>aquí</strong> This is an adverb of place, meaning "here" in English. It indicates the location in relation to the speaker.</div>','<h2>Él recogería a los niños de la escuela si tuviera auto.</h2><div><strong>Él</strong> This is a Spanish subject pronoun meaning "he". It is used here to refer to a male subject performing an action.</div><div><strong>recogería</strong> This is a verb in the conditional tense of "recoger", which means "to pick up" in English. The conditional tense expresses an action that would take place under certain conditions or circumstances.</div><div><strong>a los niños</strong> This phrase means "the children" in English. "A" is a preposition used here for the personal "a", which is used in Spanish before a direct object that is a person. "Los niños" is the direct object, representing whom the subject would pick up.</div><div><strong>de</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can mean several things, but in this context it means "from".</div><div><strong>la escuela</strong> This phrase means "the school" in English. It indicates the place from which the subject would pick up the children.</div><div><strong>si</strong> This is a conjunction in Spanish that means "if". It introduces a condition.</div><div><strong>tuviera</strong> This is the past subjunctive form of the verb "tener", which means "to have" in English. It is used here to express a hypothetical situation or condition that is contrary to fact.</div><div><strong>auto</strong> This is a noun in Spanish that means "car" in English. It denotes the object the subject wishes he had in order to perform the action.</div>','<h2>¿Cuál es la dirección del hotel?</h2><div><strong>¿Cuál</strong> This is a Spanish interrogative pronoun meaning "which" or "what". It is used here to ask for specific information, in this case, the address.</div><div><strong>es</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "ser", which means "to be". It refers to the intrinsic identity or nature of something, in this case, of the hotel’s address.</div><div><strong>la</strong> This is a Spanish definite article in its feminine singular form, equivalent to "the" in English. It precedes the noun "dirección".</div><div><strong>dirección</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "address". It is feminine and singular, matching the article "la".</div><div><strong>del</strong> This is a contraction of the Spanish preposition "de" (of/from) and the definite article "el" (the), it translates to "of the" or "from the" in English. It is used here to link the noun "dirección" with "hotel" to express possession.</div><div><strong>hotel</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "hotel". It’s a common noun and is in the singular form. In this question, it is the place for which the address is being asked.</div><div><strong>?</strong> This symbol denotes that the sentence is a question in Spanish, similarly to English.</div>','<h2>Ella tomaba fotos del paisaje.</h2><div><strong>Ella</strong> This is the third-person singular feminine pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "She" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>tomaba</strong> This is the third-person singular imperfect tense form of the verb "tomar", which means "to take". In this context, it reflects a past habitual action or a past action that was ongoing.</div><div><strong>fotos</strong> This is the plural form of "foto", which is a shortened form of "fotografía" in Spanish. It means "photos" or "pictures" in English.</div><div><strong>del</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "de" and the definite article "el", which mean "of the" or "from the" in English. It is used here to denote possession, similar to the English possessive "of the".</div><div><strong>paisaje</strong> This is a noun that means "landscape" or "scenery" in English. It is the object of the preposition "del", indicating what is being photographed.</div>','<h2>Nosotros salimos del agua para broncearnos.</h2><div><strong>Nosotros</strong> This is the first-person plural pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "we" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence, indicating that the action is performed by a group including the speaker.</div><div><strong>salimos</strong> This is the first-person plural present tense form of the verb "salir", which means "to leave" or "to go out". In this context, it suggests the action of leaving a place, specifically "the water".</div><div><strong>del agua</strong> <em>"Del" is</em> a contraction of the preposition "de" (of/from) and the definite article "el" (the), which together mean "from the" or "out of the". <em>"Agua"</em> is a Spanish noun meaning "water". Combined, <strong>del agua</strong> means "out of the water".</div><div><strong>para</strong> This is a preposition that means "for" in English. It is used here to indicate purpose or intent.</div><div><strong>broncearnos</strong> This is the reflexive form of the verb "broncear", which means "to tan" or "to bronze". The reflexive pronoun "nos" is attached to the infinitive form and translates to "ourselves", indicating that the action of tanning is being done by the subjects themselves.</div>','<h2>Ella es la menos talentosa del grupo.</h2><div><strong>Ella</strong> This is a third-person singular pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "she" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>es</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "ser", which means "to be". It is used to explain the intrinsic quality or identity of the subject.</div><div><strong>la menos talentosa</strong> This phrase consists of the definite article "la" (the), the comparative "menos" (less), and the adjective "talentosa" (talented). When combined, it means "the least talented". In this case, "la" agrees with the feminine and singular noun it’s referring to, which is implied (in this case, person).</div><div><strong>del grupo</strong> This phrase is a combination of "de" (of) and "el" (the) contracted to form "del", and "grupo" (group), meaning "of the group". The whole phrase indicates that among the members of the group, she is the least talented one.</div>','<h2>¿Cuál fue el puntaje final del partido?</h2><div><strong>¿Cuál</strong> This is a Spanish interrogative pronoun meaning "which" or "what". It is used here to ask for specific information, similar to asking "what" in English.</div><div><strong>fue</strong> This is the third-person singular past tense (preterite) of the verb "ser", which means "to be". In this context, it is used to refer to a past event, stating "was".</div><div><strong>el puntaje</strong> This is a Spanish noun composed of the masculine singular article "el" and the noun "puntaje", which means "score". It refers to the points obtained in a game or contest.</div><div><strong>final</strong> This is a Spanish adjective that means "final". It describes the noun "puntaje" to specify that the question is about the score at the end of the game.</div><div><strong>del</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "de" (of) and the masculine singular article "el" (the), which together translate to "of the" in English. It’s used to indicate possession or relation to something.</div><div><strong>partido</strong> This is a Spanish noun meaning "match" or "game". It relates to a sporting event between two teams or players.</div>','<h2>Diego es el más alto del grupo.</h2><div><strong>Diego</strong> This is a proper noun, a personal name in Spanish. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>es</strong> This is the third-person singular present tense form of the verb "ser", which means "to be" in English. It describes the state of being or identity of the subject.</div><div><strong>el más alto</strong> This phrase translates to "the tallest" in English. "El" is the masculine singular definite article "the", "más" means "more" or "most", and "alto" is an adjective meaning "tall". Alto agrees in gender and number with Diego, who is presumably male.</div><div><strong>del grupo</strong> This is a prepositional phrase. "Del" is a contraction of "de" (of) and "el" (the), together meaning "of the" or "from the". "Grupo" is a noun meaning "group". Therefore, "del grupo" translates to "of the group" or "from the group", indicating that Diego is the tallest in comparison to the others in the group.</div>','<h2>Ellos no encuentran su camino para salir del bosque.</h2><div><strong>Ellos</strong> This is the masculine plural pronoun in Spanish, equivalent to "They" in English. It’s the subject of the sentence.</div><div><strong>no</strong> This is the Spanish adverb for "no" or "not". It negates the verb that follows.</div><div><strong>encuentran</strong> This is the third-person plural of the present indicative of the verb "encontrar", which means "to find". In this sentence, it indicates an action they are unable to complete.</div><div><strong>su</strong> This is a possessive adjective that translates to "their" in English. It indicates possession or association with "camino", which is the thing they can’t find.</div><div><strong>camino</strong> In Spanish, this word means "path" or "way". It refers to the route or direction they need to find.</div><div><strong>para</strong> This is a preposition in Spanish that can be translated to "to" or "for" in this context. It is used to indicate purpose, answering the question "why?" (why do they need to find the path? - to get out of the forest).</div><div><strong>salir</strong> This is an infinitive verb that means "to leave" or "to exit". It indicates the action they want to perform in relation to the forest.</div><div><strong>del</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "de" (from) and the article "el" (the), which translates as "of the" or "from the" in English. It indicates origin or separation.</div><div><strong>bosque</strong> This Spanish word means "forest". It’s the object of the preposition "del", specifying the place they are trying to exit.</div>','<h2>He contestado todas las preguntas del cliente.</h2><div><strong>He</strong> This is the first-person singular present perfect tense auxiliary verb in Spanish, from "haber", which in this context works as a helper verb to form the present perfect tense. It means "I have".</div><div><strong>contestado</strong> This is the past participle of the verb "contestar", which means "to answer". It combines with the auxiliary verb "he" to form the present perfect tense, indicating an action that was completed in the past but is relevant to the present.</div><div><strong>todas</strong> This is the feminine plural form of the adjective "todo", which means "all" or "every". In this sentence, it modifies "preguntas" and agrees with it in gender and number.</div><div><strong>las</strong> This is the definite article "the" in its feminine plural form, matching "preguntas" which is feminine and plural.</div><div><strong>preguntas</strong> This is the plural form of "pregunta", a feminine noun meaning "questions". It is the direct object of the sentence, referring to the things that were answered.</div><div><strong>del</strong> This is a contraction of the preposition "de" (meaning "of" or "from") and the masculine singular article "el" (meaning "the"), and translates to "of the" or "from the".</div><div><strong>cliente</strong> This is a masculine singular noun meaning "client" or "customer". It specifies the possessive relationship with "las preguntas" (the questions), indicating who the questions belong to.</div>'];definitions = definitions.concat(defBonus);